actors: Browser | Server | Audit log

step: A request arrives with a cookie
msg: Browser -> Server: GET /admin/users + cookie
text: The route is admin-only, and the caller is whoever the cookie says
  they are. Nothing in the request says what role they hold, and nothing
  should.

step: First question: who is this?
note: Server: get_current_session
text: No session, or an expired one, ends here with a 401. The server doesn't
  know who you are, so asking what you may do is premature.

step: The server looks up the caller's role
note: Server: reads role from the users table
text: The role comes from the database, never from the request. A caller
  can't give themselves a role by sending one. This is what lets a role
  change take effect on the very next request.

step: Second question: may this role do this?
note: Server: role_is_authorized()
text: The route lists the roles it admits. This one function decides whether
  the caller's role is good enough. It is the function you fix in this lab,
  and everything else in the diagram already works.

step: A denial is written down with its reason
msg: Server -> Audit log: deny + both roles
text: The entry names the role the route required and the role the caller
  had. A bare 403 with no context is hard to investigate later.

step: The caller gets a 403, or the data
msg: Server -> Browser: 403 (or 200 + users)
text: A caller with no valid session got a 401 at the second step. A caller
  with a valid session and the wrong role gets a 403 here: we know exactly
  who you are, and the answer is no.
